ndent 10% low dose realizations for the different reconstruction methods. IV Experimental Setup We evaluated flow matching-based reconstruction methods using clinical brain [ 18 F ] [^{18}\mathrm{F}] FDG PET data. The experiments were run on an NVIDIA B200 GPU with 192 GB of memory. IV-A Brain PET data For the clinical data experiment, we used pretrained unconditional flow models trained on early 0-10 min frames of [ 18 F ] [^{18}\mathrm{F}] MK-6240 tau scans [ 17 ] , with an injected dose of approximately 185 MBq. These early-time-frame PET images mainly reflected cerebral blood flow information. The PET images were reconstructed using the ordered subsets EM algorithm with 3 iterations and 17 subsets, including point spread function modeling and time-of-flight information. The reconstructed images had a matrix size of 256 × 256 × 89 256	imes 256	imes 89 and a voxel size of 1.17 × 1.17 × 2.79 1.17	imes 1.17	imes 2.79 mm 3 . The PET images were subsequently downsampled and centrally cropped, resulting in a matrix size of 128 × 128 × 80 128	imes 128	imes 80 and a voxel size of 2.34 × 2.34 × 2.79 2.34	imes 2.34	imes 2.79 mm 3 . The model was implemented using a 3D U-Net backbone and trained on 116 PET datasets. Among these datasets, 110 were used for training and 6 for validation. During testing, we used 10 clinical [ 18 F ] [^{18}\mathrm{F}] FDG data from the Monash DaCRA fPET–fMRI dataset [ 41 ] . Dynamic PET scans were acquired over 90 min following an injection of approximately 238 MBq of [ 18 F ] [^{18}\mathrm{F}] FDG, and the data acquired during the 80–90 min interval were used for testing. Low dose PET data were simulated by downsampling the original list-mode data to 10% and 5% of the original counts. The 10% dose level was used for the primary evaluation, and the 5% dose level was evaluated to assess reconstruction performance under more severe low-dose conditions. The corresponding full dose data were used to generate reference images. The PET images were reconstructed on a 128 × 128 × 80 128	imes 128	imes 80 voxel grid with a voxel size of 2.34 × 2.34 × 2.79 2.34	imes 2.34	imes 2.79 mm 3 . Scatter and random corrections were performed using a voxel-driven scatter model and a maximum-likelihood method, respectively, and magnetic resonance-derived μ \mu maps were used for attenuation correction [ 42 ] . The system matrix was implemented using Parallelproj [ 43 ] . IV-B Evaluation We compared the proposed model-based PET reconstruction method with MLEM, MAPEM using the relative difference penalty [ 44 ] , decomposed diffusion sampling (DDS) [ 25 ] , and PET-FlowDPS. For a fair comparison, the diffusion model used in DDS was trained using the same training datasets and employed the same network architecture as the corresponding flow model. For the MAPEM method, we used the relative difference penalty defined as R ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) = ∑ j ∑ k ∈ N j ( x j − x k ) 2 ( x j + x k ) + γ ​ | x j − x k | , R( m{x})=\sum_{j}\sum_{k\in{N_{j}}} rac{(x_{j}-x_{k})^{2}}{(x_{j}+x_{k})+\gamma\left|x_{j}-x_{k}ight|}, (39) where N j N_{j} stands for a set of neighborhood indices of j j th voxel and γ \gamma controls the shape of the function. Following the default setting for clinical PET scanners, we set γ = 2 \gamma=2 [ 45 ] . For DDS reconstruction, we used the following iterative update: 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n + 1 ) \displaystyle\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n+1)} = 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n ) + \displaystyle=\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n)}+ 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n ) 𝑺 ​ [ 𝑨 T ​ 𝒚 𝑨 ​ 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n ) + 𝒃 − 𝑺 − 2 ​ λ DDS ​ ( 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n ) − 𝒙 ^ 0 ( 0 ) ) ] , \displaystyle rac{\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n)}}{ m{S}}\left[ m{A}^{T} rac{ m{y}}{ m{A}\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n)}+ m{b}}- m{S}-2\lambda_{	ext{DDS}}\left(\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n)}-\hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(0)}ight)ight], (40) where 𝒙 ^ 0 ( 0 ) = 𝒙 ^ 0 \hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(0)}=\hat{ m{x}}_{0} , n = 0 , 1 , … , N DDS − 1 n=0,1,\ldots,N_{	ext{DDS}}-1 denotes the number of iterations, 𝒙 ^ 0 ( n ) / 𝑺 \hat{ m{x}}_{0}^{(n)}/ m{S} is a preconditioning term and λ DDS \lambda_{	ext{DDS}} controls the strength of the regularization. We set the number of diffusion sampling steps to 200 and N DDS = 5 N_{	ext{DDS}}=5 in the experiments. For the proposed method, the flow interval was discretized into 50 sampling steps, and 10 penalized EM iterations were performed at each time point. The penalty strength was set to β = 0.1  eta=0.1 . Unless otherwise specified, these settings were used for all reported results. Mean uptake was calculated in the putamen region and background noise was evaluated using the coefficient of variation (CoV) in the white matter region as CoV = SD WM Mean WM , 	ext{CoV}= rac{	ext{SD}_{	ext{WM}}}{	ext{Mean}_{	ext{WM}}}, (41) where Mean WM 	ext{Mean}_{	ext{WM}} and SD WM 	ext{SD}_{	ext{WM}} are the mean and standard deviation values of the white matter regions, respectively. These regions were derived from FreeSurfer [ 46 ] . Peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) was also calculated using the full dose image as the reference as PSNR = 10 ​ log 10 ​ [ max ⁡ ( 𝑿 full ) 2 1 N vox ∥ 𝑿 full − 𝑿 ′ ∥ 2 2 ] , 	ext{PSNR}=10\log_{10}\Biggl[ rac{\max{( m{X_{	ext{full}}})}^{2}}{ rac{1}{N_{	ext{vox}}} igl\lVert m{X_{	ext{full}}}- m{X}^{\prime} igrVert_{2}^{2}}\Biggr], (42) where max ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \max{(\cdot)} indicates the maximum value of the image, 𝑿 full  m{X_{	ext{full}}} and 𝑿 ′  m{X}^{\prime} denote the full dose and target reconstructed images, respectively, and N vox N_{	ext{vox}} is the number of voxels. For the variability analysis, 10 independent low-dose realizations were generated by randomly sampling 10% of the events from the original listmode data. Each realization was independently reconstructed using all evaluated methods. Voxel-wise mean and standard-deviation images were calculated across the 10 reconstructed images. The bias image was calculated as the voxel-wise difference between the mean reconstructed image and the corresponding full-dose reference image. V Results Figure 2 shows the impact of the number of sampling steps K K on PSNR and on the putamen uptake–white matter CoV tradeoffs. The PSNR peaked at K = 50 K=50 and decreased thereafter. Favorable tradeoffs were observed with both 50 and 75 sampling steps. Figure 3 presents the impact of the number of penalized EM iterations on the putamen uptake–white matter CoV tradeoff curves. Ten EM iterations ( N = 10 N=10 ) provided putamen uptake closest to the full dose reference while maintaining lower white matter CoV. Therefore, K = 50 K=50 and N = 10 N=10 were used in the subsequent experiments. Figure 4 shows reconstructed images of one clinical [ 18 F ] [^{18}\mathrm{F}] FDG data using different methods. PET-FlowDPS and the proposed method produced images with lower noise levels than MLEM, MAPEM, and DDS. In particular, the proposed method provided visually higher image contrast than PET-FlowDPS, consistent with the putamen uptake–white matter CoV trade-off curves shown in Fig. 5 . DDS, PET-FlowDPS and the proposed method recovered putamen uptake comparable to that of the full dose reference. However, DDS and PET-FlowDPS exhibited less favorable noise characteristics than the proposed metho
